.TH "TDDA SAMPLE" 1 "May 2026" "3.0" "tdda sample manual"
.SH NAME
\fBtdda sample\fR — Display N random rows from a dataset

.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBtdda sample\fR [\fB-h\fR] [\fBN\fR] [\fB--seed\fR \fISEED\fP] [\fB-s\fR | \fB-S\fR]
              [\fB--fields\fR \fIFIELDS\fP] [\fB--xfields\fR \fIFIELDS\fP]
              [\fB--pandas\fR] [\fB--polars\fR] [\fB--backend\fR \fIBACKEND\fP]
              \fIINPUT\fP [\fIFIELD\fP ...]

.SH POSITIONAL ARGUMENTS
\fIINPUT\fP      Dataset path (CSV, Parquet, or colon syntax).

.PP
\fIFIELD\fP ...  Field names (or \fBfnmatch\fR wildcard patterns) to display.
           Fields appear in the order given. Equivalent to \fB--fields\fR;
           both may be combined. Wildcards must be quoted in the shell.

.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBtdda sample\fR command displays N randomly selected rows from a dataset
(default 10) as a rich table.

.PP
When no \fB--seed\fR is given, a random seed is chosen automatically and printed
so the result can be reproduced.

.PP
Null values are shown as \fB∅\fR\&.

.SH OPTIONS
\fB-h\fR, \fB-?\fR, \fB--help\fR        Show this help message and exit

.PP
\fBN\fR                       Number of random rows to show (default 10)

.PP
\fB--seed\fR \fISEED\fP             Random seed. If omitted, a seed is chosen
                          automatically and printed.

.PP
\fB--fields\fR \fIFIELDS\fP         Show only these fields. \fIFIELDS\fP is a
                          comma- or space-separated list of field names
                          or \fBfnmatch\fR wildcard patterns (e.g. \fBeu_*\fR,
                          \fB[a-z]*\fR). Fields appear in the order
                          specified. Requires quoting in the shell when
                          using spaces or wildcards.

.PP
\fB--xfields\fR \fIFIELDS\fP        Exclude these fields. Same format as
                          \fB--fields\fR\&. Fields appear in dataset order.

.PP
\fB-s\fR                        Short headers: column width driven by data;
                          headers split at word boundaries and packed
                          onto as few lines as possible.
                          See \fBtdda-cat(1)\fR for details.

.PP
\fB-S\fR                        Short headers: split anywhere to fit data width.

.PP
\fB--pandas\fR, \fB--pd\fR          Use Pandas as DataFrame engine (default)
.br
\fB--polars\fR, \fB--pl\fR          Use Polars as DataFrame engine
.br
\fB--backend\fR, \fB-B\fR \fIBACKEND\fP   Backend choice for Pandas
.br
                          \fBn\fR for numpy_nullable *
.br
                          \fBa\fR for pyarrow
.br
                          \fBo\fR for original

.SH EXAMPLES
1) \fBtdda sample accounts1k.parquet\fR

.PP
Display 10 random rows from \fBaccounts1k.parquet\fR, printing the seed used.

.PP
2) \fBtdda sample 50 accounts1k.csv:\fR

.PP
Display 50 random rows, using any associated metadata file.

.PP
3) \fBtdda sample 20 --seed 42 accounts1k.csv:\fR

.PP
Display 20 random rows with a fixed seed (reproducible).

.PP
4) \fBtdda sample --fields 'name,balance' accounts1k.csv:\fR

.PP
Display 10 random rows showing only \fBname\fR and \fBbalance\fR\&.

.PP
5) \fBtdda sample -s 20 --seed 42 accounts1k.csv:\fR

.PP
Display 20 random rows with compact multi-line headers.

